ths on freight trains with  with American mostly american-born  transient people and and there was a lot  of I had a lot of fear in  that experience because a lot of them  you know have their issues there's a lot  of violence there's a lot of addiction  with the the Mexicans I traveled with  that was not the case these are mostly  childhood friends from a little valley  and Kenneth Thoreau northwest of Mexico  City who who were there like whole  people and they're my age and it was by  and large I had a great time with them  and they looked out for me they weren't  you know I connected with a farm workers  group in Arizona and and they felt that  they needed to do right by me by looking  after me so that I've got to say of all  the different groups of people I've hung  out with that was the most fun cuz they  were guys my age you know when you've  done with the day of work you have a few  beers you yeah you're thinking a lot of  the same thoughts and and I'm still in  touch with a whole bunch of them  including that woman  Avon halika who accompanied our group of  seventeen people across the border into  Arizona and then up to Idaho and she's  back home and a bunch of others found me  on Facebook which is pretty cool  so in there's so much about that book  that is so relevant today but for me you  know if I were to recommend it to  someone today given what's going on you  know with coronavirus and questions  about which workers are essential in  which are not in the supply chain of  food and agriculture in the United  States what would be just incredible if  more people understood would be not just  you know as you describe picking oranges  up on ladders where you carry the ladder  and then you put the huge bag and then  you're throwing these oranges into the  bag which gets heavier and heavier as  you're on the ladder just bag after bag  after bag so there's the hard work of  just you go and you buy navel oranges at  a buck fifty a pound  everything that went into that first of  all but the story you tell about how  much work went in to get that job and  this is not an endorsement or attack on  crossing a border you're not authorized  to cross it's merely the empirical  reality that you know you tell the story  that starts in the mountains of Mexico  and takes bus rides and other  altercations merely to get to the border  a multi-day trip through the desert this  is all just to get a job that many of us  don't even understand that seems so  relevant today and it's just it's just  not part of most people's understanding  of what we mean when we say undocumented  workers I gotta tell you it sure  instilled in me respect for immigrants  who want to come here to work to better  themselves things were different in the  80s as you mentioned insofar as you know  it was mostly agricultural workers at  the time mostly men mostly from rural  parts of Mexico the poorest parts it's a  more diverse group now who work in all  kinds of jobs and among them there's no  denying you know are a few bad apples  but overall the people I met made me  proud to be part of a country built on  immigration you know a country populated  by people who chose to come here to make  something of themselves and that spirit  was so clear in the in the people I  worked with who by the way could work so  much harder than me I felt like such a  wuss you know complaining after a day of  picking lemons that that my hand hurt  from the Clippers or my shoulder hurt  from that heavy bag they like haha  Americans are so soft I mean along those  lines  the jobs that I'm trying to think so you  did you did the citrus part of  what would remind me which other because  I know that sometimes you were visiting  places but you weren't working like the  potato farm in Idaho what other jobs did  you work with them yeah well that was  the most extended job I had and the rest  of the time I was mostly traveling right  to get a job with a crew of cement  workers in Houston and called the boss  they had his number and they said yeah  we're gonna go to Houston and this guy  Terry all he'll give you a job and I'm  like well let's see and when he heard an  American on the line  say that I wanted a job too and he said  have you ever done it I said no no but  I'm a hard worker they'll tell you and  he goes uh you know I think I just  prefer to work with the Mexicans  obviously that's not good right I'm an  American citizen I shouldn't be locked  out but um again that's the kind of  story you can tell if you put yourself  out there so that's that's what I wanted  to do that also is by the way a reminder  of when when these conversations happen  about quote dealing with undocumented  immigration the focus on the individuals  rather than the employers seems  misguided in a sense and it sounds like  the cement boss that you're talking  about is a smaller employer but there  are very large corporations that  regularly are doing the hiring of  undocumented labor with essentially no  risk I mean sure it's in the law that  you're not supposed to do it but by and  large the large corporations are not the  ones being punished for that right right  in fact until well for many years after  I finished writing coyotes it was not a  crime to hire undocumented people only  only a crime for them to do the work so  that kind of explained things right  there we've been speaking with Ted  Conover author who practices immersion  writing I've most recently read new jack  and coyotes of his work Ted I so  appreciate your time and I I really  enjoyed reading your books and just  thank you so much for talking to me  today David it's a pleasure thank you
 

